{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: stop spending nineteen git processes on one durable act.\n\nWhy. Every act this repository files starts about nineteen git subprocesses.\nMeasured with a PATH shim on 2026-09-18, one test that files roughly 260 acts —\nTestPublishRefusesMoreThanTheBatchCeilingBeforeQueueing — started 4,967 of them,\nand the whole cmd/gs package started 95,314. The package takes between five and\nthirteen minutes, and what it is doing for most of that is process startup.\n\nPer act, by share of that test's 4,967:\n\n  1,810  36.4%  rev-parse --verify\n    782  15.7%  show\n    264   5.3%  rev-parse --show-object-format\n    263   5.3%  verify-commit\n    261   5.3%  mktree\n    261   5.3%  commit-tree\n    261   5.3%  update-ref\n    260   5.2%  cat-file --batch\n    260   5.2%  hash-object\n    259   5.2%  log\n    258   5.2%  rev-list\n\nThree of those are avoidable, and hugh has adopted all three:\n\nThe object format is asked once per act and cannot change for the life of a\nrepository. That is 264 processes in one test answering the same question.\n\nReads go out one process at a time through git show, while this repository\nalready has a batched reader — internal/gitstore's audit batch uses\ncat-file --batch — used only on the audit path.\n\nReference resolution is the largest share by far, seven per act, and much of it\nre-resolves the same reference inside one operation. Resolution is also what the\ncompare-and-swap safety rests on, so the answer is a cache whose life is one\noperation and no longer. A value remembered past the end of the operation that\nread it would be a stale frontier presented as a current one, which is the one\nthing this code may never do.\n\nThe floor is four: an act genuinely writes objects and moves a ref under\ncompare-and-swap. Reaching it by adopting fast-import or a Go git library would\ntrade away the property that git itself is the object store and the verifier,\nand this request does not ask for that.\n\nThis is not only a test cost. Every act an agent files pays the same nineteen\nstartups, on every workroom.\n\nOutcome. A durable act starts far fewer git processes, the suite that files\nthousands of them is correspondingly quicker, and nothing about what is signed,\nverified or compare-and-swapped changes.","body":{"branch":"request/git-process-cost","conditions":"1. The object format is read once per store rather than once per act, and a test pins that a second act reads it no further times. 2. The general object read path uses the batched reader this repository already has, rather than one process per object; the audit path keeps whatever it does today unless the same reader serves it. 3. Reference resolution is cached for the life of one operation and no longer. A test pins both halves: the same reference resolved twice inside one operation costs one process, and an operation that begins after another has ended sees the reference as it is then, not as it was. No cached value may cross the compare-and-swap that publishes an act. 4. The count is the evidence. Before and after figures for git processes per act and for the cmd/gs package come from the same PATH shim on the same machine, and the report states both, with the shim overhead named as the ceiling it is. 5. Nothing about what is signed, verified or compare-and-swapped changes: the projection of a real workroom log is byte-identical before and after, compared as JSON. 6. Mutation proofs for each new guard, with the three outcomes stated. 7. go vet ./... clean and go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review; ratified; landed and pushed.","target_head":"762f34ae6e5cc69ac3724aec3231edca03520479","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}